# Mutation rules for bash, for universalmutator.
#
# Format is universalmutator's own: `<python regex> ==> <replacement>`, one per
# line, backrefs as \1. An empty right-hand side deletes the match. Copied from
# the shipped static/universal.rules rather than guessed at.
#
# universal.rules is arithmetic-centric (it exists for C-like languages) and on
# shell it mostly produces junk -- it mutates the shebang into `#!+bin/bash`.
# These rules are chosen from defects THIS repo has actually shipped, so a
# survivor here means a guard that would not have caught a bug we already had.

# --- The one real find so far: `grep -Fxq` -> `grep -Fq` survived all 18 tests
# around it. Whole-line matching was load-bearing and nothing proved it.
# Generalised to dropping any single flag letter from a grep/sed/awk flag group.
# Two forms, because one is not enough. The general form requires at least one
# flag letter to REMAIN (\2 or the \1 group is non-empty), so `grep -qx` becomes
# `grep -q`. Without that constraint `grep -E` mutated to the bare `grep -`,
# where `-` is taken as the PATTERN and the real pattern as a filename -- which
# in this repo happened to still match, producing a mutant that survived for a
# reason that has nothing to do with test coverage. A junk mutant that survives
# is worse than no mutant: it reads like a finding.
(grep\s+-[A-Za-z]+)x([A-Za-z]*) ==> \1\2
(grep\s+-[A-Za-z]*)x([A-Za-z]+) ==> \1\2
(grep\s+-[A-Za-z]+)F([A-Za-z]*) ==> \1\2
(grep\s+-[A-Za-z]*)F([A-Za-z]+) ==> \1\2
(grep\s+-[A-Za-z]+)w([A-Za-z]*) ==> \1\2
(grep\s+-[A-Za-z]*)w([A-Za-z]+) ==> \1\2
(grep\s+-[A-Za-z]+)E([A-Za-z]*) ==> \1\2
(grep\s+-[A-Za-z]*)E([A-Za-z]+) ==> \1\2
# The last flag letter, dropped properly: the flag goes away rather than
# decaying into a bare `-`.
grep -E  ==> grep 
grep -F  ==> grep 
grep -x  ==> grep 

# --- The silent-success class: this repo's single most expensive defect shape.
# A failure that exits 0 and says nothing. Three of these were live at once.
exit 1 ==> exit 0
exit 2 ==> exit 0
exit 3 ==> exit 0
return 1 ==> return 0

# --- Losing the error channel. `>&2` deleted means a failure prints to stdout
# and is indistinguishable from normal output to any caller parsing it.
\s*>&2 ==>

# --- `set -e` / `pipefail` removal. arr-backup.sh exited 0 on a failed volume
# for exactly this class of reason: the status was never allowed to propagate.
set -euo pipefail ==> set -uo pipefail
set -eo pipefail ==> set -o pipefail
^(\s*)set -e\s*$ ==> \1true

# --- Negation flips. `if ! cmd` vs `if cmd` is the shape behind the
# `rc=$?`-reads-the-status-of-the-negation bug in ensure_services_running.
if ! ==> if
while ! ==> while
\[\[ -z ==> [[ -n
\[\[ -n ==> [[ -z
\[ -z ==> [ -n
\[ -n ==> [ -z
\[\[ ! ==> [[
\[ ! ==> [

# --- File-test flips. `-x` vs `-e` vs `-f` distinguish "executable" from
# "exists"; hooks-installed.bats asserted exactly these and could not fail.
-x " ==> -e "
-f " ==> -e "
-d " ==> -e "

# --- Comparison flips.
-eq ==> -ne
-ne ==> -eq
-gt ==> -ge
-lt ==> -le
-ge ==> -gt
-le ==> -lt
 == ==> !=
 != ==> ==

# --- Boolean flips.
&& ==> ||
\|\| ==> &&

# --- DISABLED, with the measurement that disabled them ------------------------
#
# These two rules are real defect classes. They are off because in the first
# sweep (2026-09-01) they produced 170 of 174 survivors between them and buried
# everything else -- the two genuine finds in that run were invisible in the
# noise until the ledger was grouped by rule.
#
# Quoting: 131 survivors. Unquoting a variable reintroduces word-splitting and
# glob expansion. Nothing in this suite kills any of them, and the reason is a
# single fact, not 131 separate gaps: no test input in tests/pre-commit-checks.bats
# contains whitespace or a glob character, so quoted and unquoted behave
# identically on every value these tests ever pass. That is one finding and it
# wants one test (a fixture path with a space in it), not 131 ledger rows.
#
# Scope: 39 survivors. Dropping `local` makes a variable global. Every one of
# these is an equivalent mutant in practice -- these helpers are called once per
# process, so a leaked global has nothing to collide with. Re-enable if a helper
# ever gains a second call site or recurses.
#
# Re-enable by uncommenting; both are one-line changes.
# "\$\{([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\}" ==> ${\1}
# "\$([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)" ==> $\1
# ^(\s*)local  ==> \1
